00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:04.470 align:start position:0%
venezuela's humanitarian catastrophe has  driven around six million people to exit  the country in the past seven years most  of them in extremely dire conditions but  as the pandemic took a toll in the  region's economy and forced millions  into unemployment and poverty some  venezuelans have started to come back to  a country that now looks very different  from the one they left behind  when alejandro rivas a 34 year old  business owner left seven years ago  venezuela offered no prospects for him  it was one of the most violent and  dangerous places in the world  according to local pollster data  analysis migration dropped 60  in 2021 from the year before  at the same time tens of thousands of  those who left many of them years ago  like rivas are returning  at the border transportation workers  claim there are still a lot of people  leaving but more are coming back the  result net migration is trending towards  zero  this is not to say that the humanitarian  crisis is over this stabilization only  came after years of economic collapse  under nicolas maduro's socialist rule  that reduced the economy to a third of  its size  22 year old danny nunes decided it was  time to return when he realized his  construction job in colombia didn't even  pay for his daughter's diapers back home  his in-laws were offering the  opportunity to help out with the grocery  store they own in downtown caracas that  was surprisingly doing better now he  says he wants to try in venezuela even  start a business of his own  yeah after leaving for colombia in 2017  32 year old javier vivenes got a cozy  job in bogota but quickly experienced a  xenophobia episode in his workplace he  decided to quit and come back to caracas  just before the pandemic together with a  close friend they saw an opportunity in  the food sector and started a small  donut business called cordonas they  quickly went from never cooking a donut  before to having people line up outside  their shop now they own three stores in  three opposite corners of the city  symbols
 

